          {
            "kind": "read",
            "raw_input": {
              "file_path": "/home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/test/experiments/README.md"
            },
            "title": "Read README.md",
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_01XG9JQdFzNYGwuMXNsecumY",
            "status": "completed",
            "raw_output": "1\t# Wave-classification experiments\n2\t\n3\tBinary classification of EUV wave events: does a LoRA fine-tune of Surya beat a\n4\trunning-difference logistic baseline, and does either improve as more images are used?\n5\t\n6\tEverything reusable lives in `workshop_infrastructure/`; everything here is either a\n7\tdriver, an analysis, or a measurement that the drivers depend on.\n8\t\n9\t## Run order\n10\t\n11\t```bash\n12\tcd <repo root>\n13\tPY=/home/jovyan/envs/surya_WS/bin/python      # the env with lightning + peft + wandb\n14\t\n15\t# Phase 1 \u2014 one data pass: warm the cache, cache the baseline's features, save 1.1 arrays\n16\tOMP_NUM_THREADS=2 $PY -m downstream_apps.test.experiments.extract_baseline_features \\\n17\t    --train-n 384 --num-workers 8\n18\t\n19\t# Phase 2 \u2014 task 1.1 figures, and whether +30 min is a good place to sample a wave\n20\t$PY -m downstream_apps.test.experiments.plot_task_1_1\n21\t$PY -m downstream_apps.test.experiments.wave_visibility_scan --n-events 6\n22\t\n23\t# Phase 3 \u2014 the baseline arm: 1.2 reproduction, learning curve, sweeps (seconds, CPU only)\n24\t$PY -m downstream_apps.test.experiments.run_baseline_study\n25\t\n26\t# Smoke test, then Phases 4-5 \u2014 LR probe and the Surya scaling study\n27\tSMOKE=1 bash downstream_apps/test/experiments/run_surya_schedule.sh\n28\tbash downstream_apps/test/experiments/run_surya_schedule.sh          # probe, then read its table\n29\tPHASES=scaling,ablations LR=<probe's pick> bash .../run_surya_schedule.sh\n30\t\n31\t# Collect everything into the headline figure and the written answer to 2.4\n32\t$PY -m downstream_apps.test.experiments.summarize_results\n33\t```\n34\t\n35\tPhase 1 must run first: Phases 2 and 3 read what it writes, and it leaves the validation\n36\tsplit on fast local storage, which is what keeps the Surya runs GPU-bound.\n37\t\n38\t## Files\n39\t\n40\t| path | responsibility |\n41\t|---|---|\n42\t| `wave_common.py` | Shared helpers: event-level stratified subsetting, the pre-flight assertions, the running-difference feature, dataset/DataLoader construction, and the local-cache plan. Imported by everything else so none of it is duplicated. |\n43\t| `extract_baseline_features.py` | Phase 1. Restartable per split. |\n44\t| `plot_task_1_1.py` | Task 1.1 \u2014 matched wave/no-wave pairs at three levels of detail. |\n45\t| `wave_visibility_scan.py` | Task 1.1 extended \u2014 running-difference contrast against onset offset. |\n46\t| `run_baseline_study.py` | Phase 3 \u2014 task 1.2, the learning curve, weight-decay and pooling sweeps. |\n47\t| `run_surya_schedule.sh` | Phases 4-5 \u2014 sequential runs, per-run wall-clock cap, continue-on-failure. |\n48\t| `summarize_results.py` | The val-loss-vs-N figure, the iso-compute view, and `task_2_4_answer.md`. |\n49\t| `results/` | Figures, metrics JSON, per-split feature caches, and `logs/`. |\n50\t\n51\tAlso outside this directory, and part of the same work:\n52\t`../4_finetune_wave_1D.py` (the fine-tuning entry point),\n53\t`../datasets/wave_dataset_memory.py` (the in-memory read path),\n54\t`../configs/config_wave_full.yaml` (run-scale config).\n55\t\n56\t## Four measurements that shaped all of the above\n57\t\n58\tEvery one of these overturned an assumption, so they are recorded rather than left in a\n59\tcommit message.\n60\t\n61\t**1. The pretrained tokenizer was silently discarded.** The checkpoint's patch embedding is\n62\t`Conv2d(26, 1280, 16, 16)` \u2014 13 channels \u00d7 **2** timesteps. The config's `time_dim: 1` built\n63\ta 13-channel one, the shapes disagreed, `load_pretrained_weights()` skipped the key, and it\n64\tstayed at its random initialization. Measured: `time_dim=1` loads 156/159 tensors,\n65\t`time_dim=2` loads 157/159 (the two missing ones are the pretraining decoder, which\n66\t`finetune=True` does not build). Every Surya result before this fix used a random tokenizer.\n67\t`wave_common.assert_tokenizer_loaded()` now blocks a run that regresses it.\n68\t\n69\t**2. LoRA never adapted attention.** `target_modules: [q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj,\n70\tfc1, fc2]` \u2014 Surya has none of those names; q/k/v are fused into `attn.qkv` and the output\n71\tprojection is `attn.proj`. PEFT raises only when *nothing* matches, so the run silently\n72\ttrained `fc1`/`fc2` alone: 2,666,241 trainable parameters where 3,157,761 was intended.\n73\t`assert_lora_adapted_attention()` blocks it.\n74\t\n75\t**3. The head was half redundant.** `head_linear` (1280\u21921280) feeds `head_unembed` (1280\u21921)\n76\twith no nonlinearity between them, so at eval the pair is exactly one linear map \u2014 1,639,680\n77\tparameters, 52% of all trainable weights, contributing zero representational capacity.\n78\t`penultimate_linear_layer: false` is now the default (2,561-parameter head, 1,518,081\n79\ttrainable in total) and the A/B is one of the ablations.\n80\t\n81\t**4. The configured cache was 40\u00d7 slower than the network.** One 0.59 GB frame:\n82\t\n83\t| destination | throughput |\n84\t|---|---|\n85\t| S3 \u2192 memory (BytesIO) | 292 MB/s single, ~400 MB/s aggregate |\n86\t| S3 \u2192 local NVMe | 352 MB/s |\n87\t| S3 \u2192 EFS scratch (the configured `s3_cache_dir`) | 8.9 MB/s, and *worse* at higher concurrency |\n88\t\n89\tThe EFS mount's throughput is a property of the filesystem \u2014 bursting mode allots ~50 KB/s\n90\tper GB stored, so ~210 MB/s for the whole 4.2 TB volume, shared with every client mounting\n91\tit. So the read path was changed to fetch whole objects into RAM and hand the buffer to\n92\th5netcdf (`../datasets/wave_dataset_memory.py`), which needs no disk at all. The local NVMe\n93\tis used only to pin the validation split, the one split re-read every epoch: measured 0.8 min\n94\tto extract val from local disk against 3.5 min streaming it.\n95\t\n96\tConcurrency then has a sharp knee, and it is `num_workers` \u2014 each worker fetches serially, so\n97\tthat *is* the number of frames in flight:\n98\t\n99\t| frames in flight | throughput | s/sample |\n100\t|---|---|---|\n101\t| 4 | 352 MB/s | 3.18 |\n102\t| 8 | **380 MB/s** | **2.97** |\n103\t| 12 | 166 MB/s | 6.74 |\n104\t\n105\tAt 12 the rate more than halves while a separate process on the same machine still reads at\n106\t424 MB/s \u2014 a concurrency knee, not a bandwidth ceiling. `--num-workers 8` puts the data path\n107\tat 2.97 s/sample against 3.11 s/sample of GPU, so the GPU is (just) the limiter. Do not raise\n108\tit to \"use the other 8 CPUs\".\n109\t",
            "locations": [
