            "title": "sed -n '1,120p' wave_common.py | cat -n",
            "status": "completed",
            "raw_output": "     1\t\"\"\"\n     2\tShared helpers for the wave-classification experiments.\n     3\t\n     4\tThree things are needed by more than one script in this directory, so they live here\n     5\tonce rather than being copied (and drifting) across the feature extractor, the baseline\n     6\tstudy and the fine-tuning entry point:\n     7\t\n     8\t1. **Event-level stratified subsetting.** The catalog stores each event twice \u2014 once as\n     9\t   ``wave`` at ``start_time + 30 min`` and once as ``no wave`` at ``start_time - 30 min``\n    10\t   \u2014 so an event is a *matched pair* of samples. Subsetting by event therefore gives\n    11\t   exact class balance for free, and never puts the two halves of one event on opposite\n    12\t   sides of a split boundary. ``waveDSDataset``'s own ``max_number_of_samples`` cannot do\n    13\t   this: it head-slices a frame sorted by ``ds_index``, so ``max_number_of_samples=10``\n    14\t   returns the 5 *earliest* pairs (all June-July 2010), which is a chronological bias, not\n    15\t   a sample.\n    16\t\n    17\t2. **Pre-flight assertions.** Every failure mode this task has actually hit is silent:\n    18\t   a tokenizer that stays random because its shape disagreed with the checkpoint, LoRA\n    19\t   target names that match nothing, a head frozen at its initialization. Each one still\n    20\t   produces a plausible-looking loss curve. These helpers turn them into startup errors.\n    21\t\n    22\t3. **The running-difference feature.** ``RunningDifferenceLogisticModel`` computes it on\n    23\t   the GPU during training; ``extract_baseline_features.py`` computes it once and caches\n    24\t   it. ``pooled_running_difference()`` is the single definition both use, and\n    25\t   ``assert_feature_matches_model()`` checks they agree numerically.\n    26\t\"\"\"\n    27\t\n    28\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n    29\t\n    30\timport os\n    31\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n    32\t\n    33\timport numpy as np\n    34\timport pandas as pd\n    35\timport torch\n    36\t\n    37\t# Column in the wave catalog that identifies an *event*. Both the \"wave\" row\n    38\t# (mid_time = start_time + 30 min) and the \"no wave\" row (mid_time - 30 min) of one\n    39\t# event share it, which is what makes pair-level subsetting possible.\n    40\tEVENT_COL = \"start_time\"\n    41\tPOSITIVE_CLASS = \"wave\"\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\tdef wave_label_transform(class_series: pd.Series) -> pd.Series:\n    45\t    \"\"\"Map the catalog's ``class`` column to float32 1.0 / 0.0.\n    46\t\n    47\t    Passed to ``waveDSDataset(label_transform=...)``. Kept here so the notebooks, the\n    48\t    feature extractor and the training script cannot disagree on which class is positive.\n    49\t    \"\"\"\n    50\t    return (class_series == POSITIVE_CLASS).astype(np.float32)\n    51\t\n    52\t\n    53\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    54\t# Event-level subsetting\n    55\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    56\t\n    57\t@dataclass\n    58\tclass SubsetInfo:\n    59\t    \"\"\"What a subsetting call actually did, for logging and assertions.\"\"\"\n    60\t    n_samples: int\n    61\t    n_events: int\n    62\t    n_positive: int\n    63\t    n_negative: int\n    64\t    n_dropped_unpaired: int\n    65\t    n_events_available: int\n    66\t\n    67\t    def describe(self) -> str:\n    68\t        return (\n    69\t            f\"{self.n_samples} samples / {self.n_events} events \"\n    70\t            f\"({self.n_positive} wave, {self.n_negative} no wave) \"\n    71\t            f\"from {self.n_events_available} complete pairs available; \"\n    72\t            f\"dropped {self.n_dropped_unpaired} unpaired sample(s)\"\n    73\t        )\n    74\t\n    75\t\n    76\tdef event_stratified_subset(\n    77\t    dataset,\n    78\t    n_samples: int | None = None,\n    79\t    seed: int = 42,\n    80\t) -> SubsetInfo:\n    81\t    \"\"\"Restrict ``dataset`` in place to a seeded, event-level stratified subset.\n    82\t\n    83\t    Only events whose *both* samples survived the Surya-index match are kept, so the\n    84\t    result is exactly 50/50 by construction and accuracy of 0.5 is exactly chance. On the\n    85\t    train split at ``ds_time_tolerance: 15min`` that costs 18 of 1210 matched samples and\n    86\t    buys perfect balance.\n    87\t\n    88\t    Args:\n    89\t        dataset: A ``waveDSDataset`` (already constructed, so the Surya-index match has\n    90\t            happened). Modified in place.\n    91\t        n_samples: Number of samples to keep \u2014 must be even, since events come in pairs.\n    92\t            ``None`` keeps every complete pair.\n    93\t        seed: Selects which events. Fixed across the runs of a scaling study so that the\n    94\t            N=48 subset is a subset of the N=96 subset is a subset of N=192, \u2026; the\n    95\t            learning curve then isolates sample count from which events were drawn.\n    96\t\n    97\t    Returns:\n    98\t        A ``SubsetInfo`` describing the selection.\n    99\t\n   100\t    Raises:\n   101\t        ValueError: if ``n_samples`` is odd, or larger than the available paired samples.\n   102\t    \"\"\"\n   103\t    df = dataset.df_valid_indices\n   104\t    if EVENT_COL not in df.columns:\n   105\t        raise ValueError(\n   106\t            f\"The wave catalog must carry a {EVENT_COL!r} column to subset by event; \"\n   107\t            f\"got columns {sorted(df.columns)[:8]}\u2026\"\n   108\t        )\n   109\t\n   110\t    # Positional order of df_valid_indices matches dataset.valid_indices, and\n   111\t    # waveDSDataset.__getitem__ indexes both by position. Any mask must be applied to\n   112\t    # both, in the same order.\n   113\t    labels = df[\"label\"].to_numpy()\n   114\t    events = df[EVENT_COL].to_numpy()\n   115\t\n   116\t    per_event = pd.Series(labels).groupby(events).nunique()\n   117\t    complete = np.sort(per_event.index[per_event == 2].to_numpy())\n   118\t    n_dropped = int(len(labels) - np.isin(events, complete).sum())\n   119\t\n   120\t    if n_samples is None:"
          },
          {
            "raw_output": "440\t    if tuple(ts.shape[1:]) != expected:\n441\t        raise AssertionError(\n442\t            f\"batch['ts'] has shape {tuple(ts.shape)}; expected (B, {', '.join(map(str, expected))}). \"\n443\t            f\"ts.shape[2] is the number of input timesteps and must be 2 for this \"\n444\t            f\"checkpoint \u2014 it comes from model.time_embedding.time_dim and \"\n445\t            f\"data.time_delta_input_minutes.\"\n446\t        )\n447\t    print(f\"[PREFLIGHT] batch['ts'] shape {tuple(ts.shape)}, dtype {ts.dtype}\")\n448\t\n449\t\n450\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n451\t# The running-difference feature\n452\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n453\t\n454\tdef pooled_running_difference(\n455\t    ts_signum_log: torch.Tensor,\n456\t    channel_index: int,\n457\t    pool_kernel: int,\n458\t) -> torch.Tensor:\n459\t    \"\"\"AIA193 running difference (now - previous), mean-pooled.\n460\t\n461\t    This is the single definition of the baseline's feature.\n462\t    ``RunningDifferenceLogisticModel.forward()`` computes it per training step;\n463\t    ``extract_baseline_features.py`` computes it once and caches the result.\n464\t\n465\t    Mean pooling composes: pooling a ``pool_kernel=8`` map by a further factor of 4 is\n466\t    identical to pooling the original by 32, because every block is the same size. That\n467\t    is what lets the cache be written once at kernel 8 (512x512, 1 MB/sample) and still\n468\t    serve the kernel-16/32/64/128 sweep exactly.\n469\t\n470\t    Args:\n471\t        ts_signum_log: ``(B, C, T, H, W)`` in **signum-log** space \u2014 z-score undone,\n472\t            log compression retained. Use ``destandardize_channels()`` to get there from\n473\t            what the dataset returns.\n474\t        channel_index: Index of AIA193 within the C dimension.\n475\t        pool_kernel: Mean-pool kernel size (and stride).\n476\t\n477\t    Returns:\n478\t        ``(B, H // pool_kernel, W // pool_kernel)``.\n479\t    \"\"\"\n480\t    x = ts_signum_log[:, channel_index, :, :, :]           # (B, T, H, W)\n481\t    diff = x[:, -1] - x[:, 0]                              # now - previous\n482\t    pooled = torch.nn.functional.avg_pool2d(diff.unsqueeze(1), kernel_size=pool_kernel)\n483\t    return pooled.squeeze(1)\n484\t\n485\t\n486\tdef repool(features: np.ndarray, factor: int) -> np.ndarray:\n487\t    \"\"\"Mean-pool cached feature maps by a further integer ``factor``.\n488\t\n489\t    Args:\n490\t        features: ``(N, S, S)`` cached at some base kernel.\n491\t        factor: Additional pooling factor; must divide ``S``.\n492\t\n493\t    Returns:\n494\t        ``(N, S // factor, S // factor)``.\n495\t    \"\"\"\n496\t    if factor == 1:\n497\t        return features\n498\t    n, s, _ = features.shape\n499\t    if s % factor:\n500\t        raise ValueError(f\"repool factor {factor} does not divide the {s}x{s} cached map.\")\n501\t    out = features.reshape(n, s // factor, factor, s // factor, factor)\n502\t    return out.mean(axis=(2, 4))\n503\t\n504\t\n505\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n506\t# Datasets and DataLoaders\n507\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n508\t\n509\tdef build_wave_datasets(\n510\t    cfg,\n511\t    scalers,\n512\t    include_test: bool = False,\n513\t    in_memory: bool = True,\n514\t    local_cache_budget_gb: float = 40.0,\n515\t):\n516\t    \"\"\"Build the train / val (/ test) ``waveDSDataset`` objects described by ``cfg``.\n517\t\n518\t    Thin wrapper over ``build_helio_datasets()`` that adds three things this task needs:\n519\t    the wave-specific keyword arguments in one place, the **test** split, and the choice of\n520\t    read path.\n521\t\n522\t    ``in_memory=True`` (the default) uses ``waveDSDatasetInMemory``, which fetches each S3\n523\t    object into RAM and hands the buffer to h5netcdf rather than writing 0.59 GB to disk\n524\t    first. On this machine that is 400 MB/s against 9 MB/s, because the configured cache\n525\t    directory is on a throughput-capped EFS mount \u2014 see that class's module docstring for\n526\t    the measurements. Set ``in_memory=False`` for the stock ``s3_mode``-driven path.\n527\t\n528\t    ``build_helio_datasets()`` returns exactly two datasets \u2014 one from\n529\t    ``data.train_data_path`` with ``phase=\"train\"`` and one from ``data.valid_data_path``\n530\t    with ``phase=\"val\"``. To get the third split we call it a second time with\n531\t    ``valid_data_path`` pointed at the test index and keep the second dataset. That is the\n532\t    right ``phase`` for a held-out split (no channel masking, no flips), and it means the\n533\t    ~20 generic dataset arguments still come from the one place that owns them.\n534\t\n535\t    Returns:\n536\t        ``(train, val)`` or ``(train, val, test)``.\n537\t    \"\"\"\n538\t    from workshop_infrastructure.datasets.builders import build_helio_datasets\n539\t\n540\t    if in_memory:\n541\t        from downstream_apps.test.datasets.wave_dataset_memory import (\n542\t            waveDSDatasetInMemory as dataset_cls,\n543\t        )\n544\t        read_kwargs = dict(\n545\t            # Populated after subsetting by pin_frames(): the whitelist has to be the\n546\t            # frames the *subset* reads, which is not known until the subset exists.\n547\t            cache_paths=None,\n548\t            local_cache_dir=cfg.data.s3_cache_dir,\n549\t            local_cache_budget_gb=local_cache_budget_gb,\n550\t        )\n551\t    else:\n552\t        from downstream_apps.test.datasets.wave_dataset import waveDSDataset as dataset_cls\n553\t        read_kwargs = {}\n554\t\n555\t    task_kwargs = dict(\n556\t        **read_kwargs,\n557\t        return_surya_stack=True,\n558\t        # None on purpose: max_number_of_samples head-slices chronologically. Subsetting\n559\t        # is event_stratified_subset()'s job, after the match has happened.\n560\t        max_number_of_samples=None,",
            "kind": "execute",
