            "raw_output": "     1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n     2\t\"\"\"\n     3\tPhase 3 \u2014 the baseline arm: task 1.2, a learning curve, and two hyperparameter sweeps.\n     4\t\n     5\tEverything here runs on the cached AIA193 running-difference features that\n     6\t``extract_baseline_features.py`` wrote, so a fit that costs ~3 s of GPU per sample when\n     7\ttrained through the live dataset costs milliseconds here. That is what turns \"does\n     8\tvalidation loss improve with more images?\" from an assertion into a measured curve.\n     9\t\n    10\tThe model is exactly ``RunningDifferenceLogisticModel``: a 32x32 mean pool of\n    11\t``AIA193(now) - AIA193(now - 12 min)`` in signum-log space, flattened into one linear layer,\n    12\ttrained with BCE-with-logits. The cache is stored at pool kernel 8 and re-pooled here,\n    13\twhich is numerically identical (mean pooling composes over equal-sized blocks) and is what\n    14\tmakes the pooling sweep free.\n    15\t\n    16\tFour experiments:\n    17\t\n    18\t1. **1.2 reproduction.** ``runs/wave_classification_baseline_running_diff/version_3``\n    19\t   trained 16,385 parameters on 10 samples at lr=0.01: train loss 0.726 -> 0.0065, train\n    20\t   accuracy 1.0 from epoch 1, validation loss rising monotonically 0.760 -> 1.006. The tell\n    21\t   that this is memorization rather than miscalibration is that validation AUROC froze at\n    22\t   exactly 0.4 from epoch 0 to 13 \u2014 the *ranking* of the validation samples never changed,\n    23\t   so the model only rescaled a direction fixed in the first few steps. Reproduced here\n    24\t   with the same 10 chronologically-earliest samples, which is what\n    25\t   ``max_samples: 10`` actually selects.\n    26\t2. **Learning curve** over N, event-level stratified and nested, several seeds per N.\n    27\t3. **Weight-decay sweep** at the largest N.\n    28\t4. **Pooling-kernel sweep** at the largest N.\n    29\t\n    30\tUsage:\n    31\t    python -m downstream_apps.test.experiments.run_baseline_study\n    32\t\"\"\"\n    33\t\n    34\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n    35\t\n    36\timport argparse\n    37\timport json\n    38\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    39\t\n    40\timport numpy as np\n    41\timport torch\n    42\t\n    43\tfrom downstream_apps.test.experiments import wave_common as wc\n    44\t\n    45\tRESULTS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / \"results\"\n    46\tBASE_POOL = 32          # the kernel RunningDifferenceLogisticModel uses\n    47\tCHANCE_BCE = float(np.log(2))\n    48\t\n    49\t\n    50\tdef parse_args() -> argparse.Namespace:\n    51\t    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__,\n    52\t                                formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)\n    53\t    p.add_argument(\"--features\", default=str(RESULTS_DIR / \"rd_features.npz\"))\n    54\t    p.add_argument(\"--epochs\", type=int, default=500,\n    55\t                   help=\"Epochs per fit. Full-batch gradient descent on <=384 samples, so \"\n    56\t                        \"these are cheap; the curve is read off the best epoch, not the last.\")\n    57\t    p.add_argument(\"--lr\", type=float, default=0.01,\n    58\t                   help=\"Matches the config's diagnostic value, which is what version_3 used.\")\n    59\t    p.add_argument(\"--seeds\", default=\"0,1,2,3,4\",\n    60\t                   help=\"Subset draws per N. Reported as median with spread.\")\n    61\t    p.add_argument(\"--curve-n\", default=\"8,16,32,64,128,256,384\")\n    62\t    p.add_argument(\"--weight-decays\", default=\"0,1e-4,1e-3,1e-2,1e-1,1\")\n    63\t    p.add_argument(\"--pool-kernels\", default=\"8,16,32,64,128\")\n    64\t    p.add_argument(\"--repro-n\", type=int, default=10,\n    65\t                   help=\"Fallback size when the cache has no 'repro' split.\")\n    66\t    p.add_argument(\"--out\", default=str(RESULTS_DIR / \"baseline_study.json\"))\n    67\t    return p.parse_args()\n    68\t\n    69\t\n    70\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    71\t# The model, and one fit\n    72\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    73\t\n    74\tdef fit_logistic(\n    75\t    X_train: np.ndarray,\n    76\t    y_train: np.ndarray,\n    77\t    X_val: np.ndarray,\n    78\t    y_val: np.ndarray,\n    79\t    epochs: int,\n    80\t    lr: float,\n    81\t    weight_decay: float = 0.0,\n    82\t    seed: int = 0,\n    83\t    batch_size: int | None = None,\n    84\t) -> dict:\n    85\t    \"\"\"Fit the linear readout and return its per-epoch history plus the best-epoch metrics.\n    86\t\n    87\t    ``batch_size=None`` means full-batch gradient descent, which is the right default for the\n    88\t    learning curve: on <=384 samples it optimizes the same objective with less gradient noise,\n    89\t    so a change in the curve is a property of the data rather than of the batch schedule.\n    90\t    Pass an explicit ``batch_size`` to reproduce a specific Lightning run, whose minibatch\n    91\t    schedule is part of what produced its behaviour.\n    92\t    \"\"\"\n    93\t    from sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score\n    94\t\n    95\t    torch.manual_seed(seed)\n    96\t    n_features = X_train.shape[1] * X_train.shape[2]\n    97\t    model = torch.nn.Linear(n_features, 1)\n    98\t    opt = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=lr, weight_decay=weight_decay)\n    99\t    loss_fn = torch.nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss()\n   100\t\n   101\t    xt = torch.from_numpy(X_train.reshape(len(X_train), -1))\n   102\t    yt = torch.from_numpy(y_train).unsqueeze(1)\n   103\t    xv = torch.from_numpy(X_val.reshape(len(X_val), -1))\n   104\t    yv = torch.from_numpy(y_val).unsqueeze(1)\n   105\t\n   106\t    generator = torch.Generator().manual_seed(seed)\n   107\t    history = []\n   108\t    for epoch in range(epochs):\n   109\t        model.train()\n   110\t        if batch_size is None:\n   111\t            opt.zero_grad()\n   112\t            out = model(xt)\n   113\t            loss = loss_fn(out, yt)\n   114\t            loss.backward()\n   115\t            opt.step()\n   116\t        else:\n   117\t            order = torch.randperm(len(xt), generator=generator)\n   118\t            for start in range(0, len(order), batch_size):\n   119\t                sel = order[start: start + batch_size]\n   120\t                opt.zero_grad()",
            "status": "completed",
