            "raw_input": {
              "description": "Design scalar RD logistic baseline",
              "prompt": "Repo: /home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026 (branch downstream/wave_classification). Read CLAUDE.md at repo root first \u2014 clarity/reusability/simplicity are the primary directives and this repo is a teaching template.\n\nGOAL the user asked for: `downstream_apps/test/models/simple_baseline.py::RunningDifferenceLogisticModel` currently mean-pools an AIA193 running difference by 32x32 and feeds the whole 128x128 pooled map into `nn.Linear(16384, 1)`. The user wants the logistic regression to operate on a **scalar** instead: compute the 32x32 mean pool of the running difference, take **abs()** of the pooled map, then **average over space** to collapse it to one number per sample, and run logistic regression on that.\n\nFacts I have already established (do not re-derive, but do verify anything you plan to touch):\n\n- `downstream_apps/test/models/simple_baseline.py:101-140` \u2014 current class. Note `forward()` ends with `self.linear(x)` where `x` is `(B, 1, H', W')` and `linear.in_features = pooled_side**2` \u2014 there is a **missing flatten**, so the current code would raise a shape error. Confirm this.\n- `downstream_apps/test/experiments/wave_common.py:454-484` `pooled_running_difference(ts_signum_log, channel_index, pool_kernel) -> (B, S, S)` is documented as \"the single definition of the baseline's feature\", shared by the model and by `extract_baseline_features.py`. `wave_common.py:486+` has `repool(features, factor)`.\n- `downstream_apps/test/4_finetune_wave_1D.py:346-364` constructs the model under `--train_baseline` with `channel_index=cfg.data.channels.index(\"aia193\")`, `img_size=cfg.model.img_size`, and a `destandardize_channels` preprocess_fn.\n- `downstream_apps/test/experiments/extract_baseline_features.py:118-130` caches pooled maps at `--pool-kernel` (default documented as 8, 512x512, so the kernel-16/32/64/128 sweep is exact because mean pooling composes).\n- `downstream_apps/test/experiments/run_baseline_study.py:73-160` `fit_logistic()` builds `torch.nn.Linear(X.shape[1]*X.shape[2], 1)` from cached maps; there is a learning curve, a weight-decay sweep, and a **pooling-kernel sweep** at `run_baseline_study.py:277-296`. `summarize_results.py` consumes `baseline_study.json`.\n\nKEY numerical point to reason about and state explicitly in the plan: mean pooling composes, so pool-32 can be recovered exactly from cached pool-8 maps \u2014 but `abs()` does NOT compose. The scalar must be `mean(|pool32(diff)|)`, i.e. abs applied AFTER pooling to the target kernel and BEFORE the spatial mean. Cached pool-8 features can still serve exactly, provided the study script repools to K first, then abs, then mean. Verify this holds for the sweep code path.\n\nWhat I need from you \u2014 a concrete implementation plan covering:\n\n1. The change to `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel`: exact new `forward()`, what `__init__` args become (does `img_size` still matter? `nn.Linear(1, 1)` = 2 params), and whether the feature computation should be delegated to / shared with `wave_common.pooled_running_difference()` or whether that helper belongs in the model module. Note the dependency direction: `experiments/` imports from `models/`, so a helper living in `experiments/wave_common.py` cannot be imported by the model without inverting that. Recommend where the single definition of the scalar feature should live given CLAUDE.md's reusability directive, and keep the change minimal.\n2. Whether to keep the old full-map variant reachable (a flag, a second class, or delete it) \u2014 argue one way, considering that `run_baseline_study.py`'s pooling sweep and the recorded 16,385-parameter result in the README/docstrings reference the map variant.\n3. Feature-scale concern: a single raw scalar into `nn.Linear(1,1)` \u2014 the existing `lr=0.01` and weight-decay sweep were tuned for a 16k-dim input. Assess whether the scalar needs standardization (fixed affine from train stats, or none) and what the honest minimal-complexity recommendation is. Weight decay on a 2-parameter model is nearly meaningless \u2014 say so if true.\n4. Ripple effects: exactly which call sites, docstrings, config fields, README/ADAPTING.md passages, and JSON schema keys change; whether `extract_baseline_features.py` needs any change at all (I believe not, if the cache stays at pool-8 maps); how `run_baseline_study.py::fit_logistic` and the pooling sweep should adapt.\n5. Verification: the cheapest end-to-end checks (a tiny torch shape test on synthetic `(B, C, T, H, W)`, an equality check that mean(|repool(pool8,4)|) computed from the cache matches the model's scalar on the same sample, and the `--train_baseline` smoke run with capped max_samples).\n\nBe concrete with code snippets. Report file:line for everything you propose changing. Do not edit any files \u2014 plan mode is active.",
