            "raw_output": "File created successfully at: /home/jovyan/.claude/plans/inside-surya-workshop2026-downstream-app-polymorphic-moon.md (file state is current in your context \u2014 no need to Read it back)",
            "status": "completed",
            "raw_input": {
              "content": "# Scalar-feature running-difference logistic baseline\n\n## Context\n\n`RunningDifferenceLogisticModel` (`downstream_apps/test/models/simple_baseline.py:101`) is the\nnon-Surya arm of the wave-classification study. Today it mean-pools\n`AIA193(now) - AIA193(prev)` by 32\u00d732 and feeds the **whole 128\u00d7128 pooled map** into\n`nn.Linear(16384, 1)` \u2014 16,385 parameters fit on \u2264384 samples, which is what\n`run_baseline_study.py` measured as memorization (train loss \u2192 0.0065, val AUROC frozen at\n0.4).\n\nVisual inspection in `experiments/` showed the 32\u00d732 pool is where the wave front becomes\nprominent, so the *feature* is good; the *readout* is what has too many degrees of freedom.\nThe user wants the logistic regression to operate on a **scalar**: 32\u00d732 mean pool of the\nrunning difference \u2192 `abs()` \u2192 spatial mean \u2192 one number per sample. That turns a\n16,385-parameter map fit into a 2-parameter fit of a physically interpretable\n\"how much did AIA193 change anywhere on the disk\" statistic.\n\nA latent bug is fixed as a side effect: `simple_baseline.py:139-140` calls\n`self.linear(x)` on a `(B, 1, H', W')` tensor whose last dim is `W'=128`, not\n`in_features=16384`, so the current `--train_baseline` path would raise a shape error.\n\n## Ordering matters: `abs` does not compose, mean pooling does\n\n`experiments/wave_common.py:454` documents that mean pooling composes, which is why the\nfeature cache is written once at kernel 8 (512\u00d7512) and re-pooled to serve the\nkernel-16/32/64/128 sweep exactly. `abs()` breaks that: `mean(|pool8|) \u2260 mean(|pool32|)`.\n\nThe feature is therefore defined as **`mean( |pool_K(diff)| )`** \u2014 abs applied *after*\npooling to the target kernel `K`, *before* the spatial mean. The existing pool-8 cache still\nserves every kernel exactly, provided the study script repools to `K` first, then takes\n`abs`, then the spatial mean. No re-extraction pass is needed.\n\n## Consequence worth stating up front\n\nA logistic regression on one feature is monotone in that feature, so **ranking metrics\n(AUROC) are a fixed property of the feature**, invariant to the learned weight up to sign.\nOnly the loss and the decision threshold are actually learned. Two things follow:\n\n- The model becomes a threshold detector; its AUROC can be computed in closed form from the\n  cached features without training at all \u2014 a useful sanity check to add.\n- Weight decay on 2 parameters is close to meaningless. The weight-decay sweep in\n  `run_baseline_study.py` should be reported for the scalar arm but not read as regularization\n  in the same sense as for the 16k-dim map.\n\n## Changes\n\n### 1. `downstream_apps/test/models/simple_baseline.py` \u2014 the feature and the model\n\nAdd one module-level function that is the single definition of the scalar feature, so the\nmodel and the cached-feature study cannot drift apart:\n\n```python\ndef mean_abs_pooled_running_difference(\n    ts_signum_log: torch.Tensor, channel_index: int, pool_kernel: int\n) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"|32x32 mean pool of the running difference|, averaged over the disk. (B, 1).\"\"\"\n    x = ts_signum_log[:, channel_index]                                  # (B, T, H, W)\n    diff = x[:, -1] - x[:, 0]                                            # now - previous\n    pooled = F.avg_pool2d(diff.unsqueeze(1), kernel_size=pool_kernel)    # (B, 1, S, S)\n    return pooled.abs().mean(dim=(-2, -1))                               # (B, 1)\n```\n\nReturning `(B, 1)` feeds `nn.Linear(1, 1)` directly \u2014 no `rearrange`, no flatten to get wrong.\n\nRewrite the class:\n\n```python\nclass RunningDifferenceLogisticModel(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, channel_index: int, pool_kernel: int = 32):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.channel_index = channel_index\n        self.pool_kernel = pool_kernel\n        self.linear = nn.Linear(1, 1)          # 2 parameters\n\n    def forward(self, batch: dict) -> torch.Tensor:\n        feature = mean_abs_pooled_running_difference(\n            batch[\"ts\"], self.channel_index, self.pool_kernel\n        )\n        return self.linear(feature)\n```\n\n`img_size` is no longer needed \u2014 the readout width no longer depends on it. Drop it from\n`__init__` rather than accepting and ignoring it. Update the class docstring: the `T=2`\nordering `[t_prev, t_now]` contract and the signum-log-space requirement both still hold and\nmust stay documented.\n\n### 2. `downstream_apps/test/4_finetune_wave_1D.py:355-358` \u2014 call site\n\nDrop the `img_size=cfg.model.img_size` argument. The `destandardize_channels` preprocess_fn\nand the `WaveLightningModule` wrapper are unchanged. The `[MODEL]` print will now report\n2 parameters instead of 16,385.\n\n### 3. `downstream_apps/test/experiments/wave_common.py:454-484` \u2014 cross-reference\n\n`pooled_running_difference()` still has a job: `extract_baseline_features.py` and the task-1.1\nfigures need the **maps**. Keep it as-is, but update its docstring \u2014 it currently claims to be\nthe definition `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel.forward()` computes, which is no longer true.\nIt is now the map the scalar is *derived* from; add the \"abs does not compose\" warning so\nnobody forms the scalar from a cache at the wrong kernel.\n\n### 4. `downstream_apps/test/experiments/run_baseline_study.py` \u2014 the cached-feature study\n\n- `features(split, kernel)` (around `run_baseline_study.py:200`) currently returns `(N, S, S)`.\n  Add the scalar reduction \u2014 `np.abs(repooled).mean(axis=(1, 2))[:, None]` \u2192 `(N, 1)` \u2014 so it\n  matches what the model computes. Keep the map form reachable for the sweep comparison.\n- `fit_logistic()` (`run_baseline_study.py:73`) computes\n  `n_features = X_train.shape[1] * X_train.shape[2]`, which assumes 3-D. Change to\n  `X_train.reshape(len(X_train), -1).shape[1]` so it accepts both `(N, S, S)` and `(N, 1)`.\n- Add a closed-form AUROC of the scalar feature (`roc_auc_score(y_val, feature)`) alongside\n  each fit \u2014 free, and it is the number that actually characterizes the feature.\n- Update the module docstring (`run_baseline_study.py:10-15`, which describes \"flattened into\n  one linear layer\") and the 1.2-reproduction narrative that references the\n  16,385-parameter fit. The reproduction of the *old* run should stay described as history,\n  clearly labeled as the map variant, since it is a recorded measurement.\n- The pooling sweep (`run_baseline_study.py:277-296`) stays meaningful and gets more\n  interesting: kernel now changes the scalar itself, not the feature count.\n\n### 5. Docs and config comments\n\n- `downstream_apps/test/configs/config_wave_full.yaml:9` and `:149` \u2014 comments that call it\n  \"the 16 K-param logistic baseline\". The `lr: 0.01` there was chosen for a 16k-dim input.\n- `downstream_apps/test/experiments/README.md` \u2014 the \"Files\" table entry for\n  `run_baseline_study.py` and the headline description.\n- `downstream_apps/test/experiments/plot_task_1_1.py:10` \u2014 its docstring says `pooled_32` is\n  \"what `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel` actually consumes\"; the model now consumes one scalar\n  derived from it. Cheap and worthwhile: annotate each panel with the sample's scalar value, so\n  the figure shows the number the classifier sees.\n- `downstream_apps/test/ADAPTING.md:35` names `simple_baseline.py`'s contents \u2014 check whether\n  the parameter count appears there too.\n\n### 6. Learning rate\n\n`lr: 0.01` was tuned for 16k inputs. On a single raw feature of unknown magnitude, `nn.Linear(1,1)`\ndefault init plus that lr may converge slowly, but **AUROC is unaffected** \u2014 only calibration\nis. Recommendation: leave the config lr alone, print the train-split feature mean/std at\nstartup, and let the study script's cheap fits (`--lr`) find the value. Do **not** add feature\nstandardization unless the printed statistics show it is needed; see Open question 3.\n\n## Verification\n\n1. **Shape and value test, CPU, seconds.** Synthetic `ts` of shape `(4, 1, 2, 128, 128)`:\n   assert the model output is `(4, 1)`, that `sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters()) == 2`,\n   and that a hand-computed `mean(|avg_pool2d(diff, 32)|)` matches\n   `mean_abs_pooled_running_difference` to float tolerance. Consider adding this to\n   `tests/` alongside `tests/test_lora_setup.py` (also CPU-only and fast).\n2. **Cache-consistency check.** For one cached sample, assert\n   `mean(|repool(pooled_8, 4)|)` equals the model's scalar computed from the full-resolution\n   `running_diff` array saved in `results/figures/*.npz`. This is the check that the\n   \"abs after repool\" ordering is right.\n3. **Non-composition guard.** Assert `mean(|pool8|) != mean(|pool32|)` on real data, so the\n   distinction is pinned rather than only commented.\n4. **Study script.** `PY=/home/jovyan/envs/surya_WS/bin/python;\n   $PY -m downstream_apps.test.experiments.run_baseline_study` \u2014 runs on the existing cache in\n   seconds, no GPU, no re-extraction. Compare `best_val_auroc` against the closed-form feature\n   AUROC and against `ln 2 = 0.6931` for `ConstantProbabilityModel`.\n5. **End-to-end smoke.** `--train_baseline` with `max_samples` capped in the YAML:\n   `$PY -m downstream_apps.test.4_finetune_wave_1D --train_baseline --max-epochs 2 --no-wandb`.\n   Confirms the Lightning path, the `destandardize_channels` preprocess_fn, and the `[MODEL]`\n   parameter count. This is also the first run of that path that does not hit the flatten bug.\n\n## Open questions for the user\n\n1. **Scope.** Model only, or the cached-feature study (\u00a74) and docs (\u00a75) too? Left as written,\n   the plan does all of it so the study and the model measure the same thing.\n2. **One scalar, or a small vector?** As specified this is exactly one number from AIA193,\n   giving a 2-parameter monotone threshold detector. If more capacity is wanted while staying\n   \"scalars, not images\", the natural extensions are one scalar per channel (13 features) or a\n   few spatial statistics of the same pooled map (mean|\u00b7|, max|\u00b7|, 95th percentile \u2192 3-4\n   features). Say which you want; the plan assumes the literal one-scalar version.\n3. **Feature standardization.** Raw scalar into `nn.Linear(1,1)` (current plan), or standardize\n   with train-split statistics? It changes optimization speed and calibration only, never AUROC.\n4. **Keep the map variant?** The plan replaces it in the model and keeps it reachable in the\n   study script for comparison. Alternative: a second class, or a `readout: {map, scalar}` flag.\n",
              "file_path": "/home/jovyan/.claude/plans/inside-surya-workshop2026-downstream-app-polymorphic-moon.md"
