            "status": "completed",
            "kind": "read",
            "raw_output": "1\t# Scalar-feature running-difference logistic baseline\n2\t\n3\t## Context\n4\t\n5\t`RunningDifferenceLogisticModel` (`downstream_apps/test/models/simple_baseline.py:101`) is the\n6\tnon-Surya arm of the wave-classification study. Today it mean-pools\n7\t`AIA193(now) - AIA193(prev)` by 32\u00d732 and feeds the **whole 128\u00d7128 pooled map** into\n8\t`nn.Linear(16384, 1)` \u2014 16,385 parameters fit on \u2264384 samples, which is what\n9\t`run_baseline_study.py` measured as memorization (train loss \u2192 0.0065, val AUROC frozen at\n10\t0.4).\n11\t\n12\tVisual inspection in `experiments/` showed the 32\u00d732 pool is where the wave front becomes\n13\tprominent, so the *feature* is good; the *readout* is what has too many degrees of freedom.\n14\tThe user wants the logistic regression to operate on a **scalar**: 32\u00d732 mean pool of the\n15\trunning difference \u2192 `abs()` \u2192 spatial mean \u2192 one number per sample. That turns a\n16\t16,385-parameter map fit into a 2-parameter fit of a physically interpretable\n17\t\"how much did AIA193 change anywhere on the disk\" statistic.\n18\t\n19\tA very recent regression is fixed as a side effect: `simple_baseline.py:139-140` calls\n20\t`self.linear(x)` on a `(B, 1, H', W')` tensor whose last dim is `W'=128`, not\n21\t`in_features=16384`, so the current `--train_baseline` path raises\n22\t`mat1 and mat2 shapes cannot be multiplied`. The stale bytecode in\n23\t`models/__pycache__/simple_baseline.cpython-312.pyc` still contains `flatten(start_dim=\u2026)`\n24\tin that forward, and `1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb` has working output from it \u2014 so the\n25\tflatten was dropped in the uncommitted 19:05 edit. The scalar rewrite removes the flatten\n26\tentirely rather than restoring it.\n27\t\n28\tLine 138's `# (B, T, H, W)` comment is also wrong (after `x[:, -1] - x[:, 0]` the tensor is\n29\t`(B, H, W)`), and the file has no trailing newline.\n30\t\n31\t## Ordering matters: `abs` does not compose, mean pooling does\n32\t\n33\t`experiments/wave_common.py:454` documents that mean pooling composes, which is why the\n34\tfeature cache is written once at kernel 8 (512\u00d7512) and re-pooled to serve the\n35\tkernel-16/32/64/128 sweep exactly. `abs()` breaks that: `mean(|pool8|) \u2260 mean(|pool32|)`.\n36\t\n37\tThe feature is therefore defined as **`mean( |pool_K(diff)| )`** \u2014 abs applied *after*\n38\tpooling to the target kernel `K`, *before* the spatial mean. The existing pool-8 cache still\n39\tserves every kernel exactly, provided the study script repools to `K` first, then takes\n40\t`abs`, then the spatial mean. No re-extraction pass is needed.\n41\t\n42\t## Consequence worth stating up front\n43\t\n44\tA logistic regression on one feature is monotone in that feature, so **ranking metrics\n45\t(AUROC) are a fixed property of the feature**, invariant to the learned weight up to sign.\n46\tOnly the loss and the decision threshold are actually learned. Two things follow:\n47\t\n48\t- The model becomes a threshold detector; its AUROC can be computed in closed form from the\n49\t  cached features without training at all \u2014 a useful sanity check to add.\n50\t- Weight decay on 2 parameters is close to meaningless. The weight-decay sweep in\n51\t  `run_baseline_study.py` should be reported for the scalar arm but not read as regularization\n52\t  in the same sense as for the 16k-dim map.\n53\t\n54\t## Changes\n55\t\n56\t### 1. `downstream_apps/test/models/simple_baseline.py` \u2014 the feature and the model\n57\t\n58\tAdd one module-level function that is the single definition of the scalar feature, so the\n59\tmodel and the cached-feature study cannot drift apart:\n60\t\n61\t```python\n62\tdef mean_abs_pooled_running_difference(\n63\t    ts_signum_log: torch.Tensor, channel_index: int, pool_kernel: int\n64\t) -> torch.Tensor:\n65\t    \"\"\"|32x32 mean pool of the running difference|, averaged over the disk. (B, 1).\"\"\"\n66\t    x = ts_signum_log[:, channel_index]                                  # (B, T, H, W)\n67\t    diff = x[:, -1] - x[:, 0]                                            # now - previous\n68\t    pooled = F.avg_pool2d(diff.unsqueeze(1), kernel_size=pool_kernel)    # (B, 1, S, S)\n69\t    return pooled.abs().mean(dim=(-2, -1))                               # (B, 1)\n70\t```\n71\t\n72\tReturning `(B, 1)` feeds `nn.Linear(1, 1)` directly \u2014 no `rearrange`, no flatten to get wrong.\n73\t`RegressionFlareModel.forward` (`simple_baseline.py:71`) already uses exactly this\n74\t`abs().mean(spatial dims)` idiom, so the two baselines in this file stay stylistically of a\n75\tpiece. `metrics/template_metrics.py` does `preds.reshape(-1)`, so a `(B, 1)` output needs no\n76\tmetric change.\n77\t\n78\tRewrite the class:\n79\t\n80\t```python\n81\tclass RunningDifferenceLogisticModel(nn.Module):\n82\t    def __init__(self, channel_index: int, pool_kernel: int = 32):\n83\t        super().__init__()\n84\t        self.channel_index = channel_index\n85\t        self.pool_kernel = pool_kernel\n86\t        self.linear = nn.Linear(1, 1)          # 2 parameters\n87\t\n88\t    def forward(self, batch: dict) -> torch.Tensor:\n89\t        feature = mean_abs_pooled_running_difference(\n90\t            batch[\"ts\"], self.channel_index, self.pool_kernel\n91\t        )\n92\t        return self.linear(feature)\n93\t```\n94\t\n95\t`img_size` is no longer needed \u2014 the readout width no longer depends on it. Drop it from\n96\t`__init__` rather than accepting and ignoring it. Update the class docstring: the `T=2`\n97\tordering `[t_prev, t_now]` contract (guaranteed by `helio.py:_get_index_data`, which always\n98\tputs the offset-0 frame last) and the signum-log-space requirement both still hold and must\n99\tstay documented. Fix the stale `# (B, T, H, W)` comment and the missing trailing newline.\n100\t\n101\t### 2. `downstream_apps/test/4_finetune_wave_1D.py:355-358` \u2014 call site\n102\t\n103\tDrop the `img_size=cfg.model.img_size` argument. The `destandardize_channels` preprocess_fn\n104\tand the `WaveLightningModule` wrapper are unchanged. The `[MODEL]` print will now report\n105\t2 parameters instead of 16,385.\n106\t\n107\t### 3. `downstream_apps/test/experiments/wave_common.py:454-484` \u2014 cross-reference\n108\t\n109\t`pooled_running_difference()` still has a job: `extract_baseline_features.py` and the task-1.1\n110\tfigures need the **maps**. Keep it as-is, but update its docstring \u2014 it currently claims to be\n111\tthe definition `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel.forward()` computes, which is no longer true.\n112\tIt is now the map the scalar is *derived* from; add the \"abs does not compose\" warning so\n113\tnobody forms the scalar from a cache at the wrong kernel.\n114\t\n115\t**Also implement the guard that is already documented but missing.** `wave_common.py:25` and\n116\t`experiments/README.md:42` both promise an `assert_feature_matches_model()` that checks the\n117\tcached feature agrees numerically with what the model computes \u2014 `grep` finds no such\n118\tfunction. That absence is exactly why the dropped `flatten` went unnoticed. Add it here\n119\talongside the other pre-flight assertions (`assert_batch_shape` at `wave_common.py:436`,\n120\t`assert_trainable_count` at `:411`): run `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel`'s feature function on\n121\tone live batch, compare against `abs(repool(cached_pool8, K)).mean()` for the same\n122\t`ds_index`, and raise with both numbers if they differ beyond float tolerance. This is the\n123\tone addition that pays for itself.\n124\t\n125\t### 4. `downstream_apps/test/experiments/run_baseline_study.py` \u2014 the cached-feature study\n126\t\n127\t- `features(split, kernel)` at `run_baseline_study.py:200-201` is a two-line closure and the\n128\t  single place the cache is turned into a design matrix \u2014 the whole change lands here:\n129\t  `np.abs(wc.repool(...)).mean(axis=(1, 2))[:, None]` \u2192 `(N, 1)`. Give it a `reduce: bool`\n130\t  parameter (or a module-level `SCALAR_FEATURE` switch) so the map form stays reachable, which\n131\t  is what makes the scalar-vs-map comparison a measurement rather than a claim.\n132\t- `fit_logistic()` (`run_baseline_study.py:73`) computes\n133\t  `n_features = X_train.shape[1] * X_train.shape[2]`, which assumes 3-D. Change to\n134\t  `X_train.reshape(len(X_train), -1).shape[1]` so it accepts both `(N, S, S)` and `(N, 1)`.\n135\t- Add a closed-form AUROC of the scalar feature (`roc_auc_score(y_val, feature)`) alongside\n136\t  each fit \u2014 free, and it is the number that actually characterizes the feature.\n137\t- Update the module docstring (`run_baseline_study.py:10-15`, which describes \"flattened into\n138\t  one linear layer\") and the 1.2-reproduction narrative that references the\n139\t  16,385-parameter fit. The reproduction of the *old* run should stay described as history,\n140\t  clearly labeled as the map variant, since it is a recorded measurement.\n141\t- The pooling sweep (`run_baseline_study.py:277-296`) stays meaningful and gets more\n142\t  interesting: kernel now changes the scalar itself, not the feature count.\n143\t\n144\t### 5. Docs and config comments\n145\t\n146\t- `downstream_apps/test/configs/config_wave_full.yaml:9` and `:149` \u2014 comments that call it\n147\t  \"the 16 K-param logistic baseline\". The `lr: 0.01` there was chosen for a 16k-dim input.\n148\t- `downstream_apps/test/experiments/README.md` \u2014 the \"Files\" table entry for\n149\t  `run_baseline_study.py` and the headline description.\n150\t- `downstream_apps/test/experiments/plot_task_1_1.py:10` \u2014 its docstring says `pooled_32` is\n151\t  \"what `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel` actually consumes\"; the model now consumes one scalar\n152\t  derived from it. Cheap and worthwhile: annotate each panel with the sample's scalar value, so\n153\t  the figure shows the number the classifier sees. Its contrast statistic at\n154\t  `plot_task_1_1.py:148-152` is `abs(pooled_32).max()` \u2014 the model's feature is\n155\t  `abs(pooled_32).mean()`, one line away, and reporting the *mean* is what now corresponds to\n156\t  the classifier's input. Report both, since the max is what the eye picks out of the figure.\n157\t- `downstream_apps/test/experiments/wave_visibility_scan.py:129-144` \u2014 `contrast_stats()`\n158\t  returns `pooled_max`. Add `pooled_mean_abs` so the onset-offset scan measures the separability\n159\t  of the actual feature, which is the whole point of that scan.\n160\t- `downstream_apps/test/ADAPTING.md:35` names `simple_baseline.py`'s contents \u2014 check whether\n161\t  the parameter count appears there too.\n162\t\n163\t### 6. Learning rate \u2014 this one is not cosmetic\n164\t\n165\t`lr: 0.01` was tuned for a 16k-dimensional input. The scalar is a mean of absolute\n166\tsignum-log differences, so it is small \u2014 order 0.1 \u2014 while `nn.Linear(1, 1)` initializes\n167\t`w ~ U(-1, 1)`. To move the logit by a useful amount the weight has to reach order 10-100,\n168\tand Adam moves a parameter by roughly `lr` per step, so at `lr=0.01` that is thousands of\n169\tsteps. The 500-epoch full-batch fits in `run_baseline_study.py` would be reading a curve\n170\tthat has not converged.\n171\t\n172\tTwo fixes, and the plan does both because they serve different call paths:\n173\t\n174\t- **Study script:** z-score the scalar using **train-split statistics only** (fit on the\n175\t  subset, applied to train and val). This is ordinary practice for logistic regression, keeps\n176\t  the pooling sweep comparable across kernels whose scalars have different magnitudes, and\n177\t  costs three lines. Record the mean/std in `baseline_study.json`.\n178\t- **Lightning arm:** raise `learning_rate` for the `--train_baseline` run with a comment\n179\t  saying why, and print the batch's feature mean/std once at startup so the choice is\n180\t  visible rather than folklore.\n181\t\n182\tNote again that **none of this moves AUROC** \u2014 a 1-feature logistic regression is monotone in\n183\tits feature. It moves the loss, which is what `ModelCheckpoint` monitors, so it does matter\n184\tfor anything comparing `val_loss` against the Surya arm.\n185\t\n186\t## Verification\n187\t\n188\t1. **Shape and value test, CPU, seconds.** Synthetic `ts` of shape `(4, 1, 2, 128, 128)`:\n189\t   assert the model output is `(4, 1)`, that `sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters()) == 2`,\n190\t   and that a hand-computed `mean(|avg_pool2d(diff, 32)|)` matches\n191\t   `mean_abs_pooled_running_difference` to float tolerance. Consider adding this to\n192\t   `tests/` alongside `tests/test_lora_setup.py` (also CPU-only and fast).\n193\t2. **Cache-consistency check \u2014 the `assert_feature_matches_model()` from \u00a73.** For one cached\n194\t   sample, assert `mean(|repool(pooled_base, 4)|)` equals the model's scalar computed from the\n195\t   full-resolution `running_diff` in `results/figure_arrays/*.npz`. This is the check that the\n196\t   \"abs after repool\" ordering is right, and it is the guard whose absence let the `flatten`\n197\t   regression through.\n198\t3. **Non-composition guard.** Assert `mean(|pool8|) != mean(|pool32|)` on real data, so the\n199\t   distinction is pinned rather than only commented.\n200\t   Watch the channel index while doing this: it is **3** in the 13-channel configs but **0** in\n201\t   `extract_baseline_features.py`, which overrides `cfg.data.channels = [\"aia193\"]`. The\n202\t   `cfg.data.channels.index(\"aia193\")` lookup at the call site already handles both \u2014 do not\n203\t   replace it with a literal.\n204\t4. **Study script.** `PY=/home/jovyan/envs/surya_WS/bin/python;\n205\t   $PY -m downstream_apps.test.experiments.run_baseline_study` \u2014 runs on the existing cache in\n206\t   seconds, no GPU, no re-extraction. Compare `best_val_auroc` against the closed-form feature\n207\t   AUROC and against `ln 2 = 0.6931` for `ConstantProbabilityModel`.\n208\t5. **End-to-end smoke.** `--train_baseline` with `max_samples` capped in the YAML:\n209\t   `$PY -m downstream_apps.test.4_finetune_wave_1D --train_baseline --max-epochs 2 --no-wandb`.\n210\t   Confirms the Lightning path, the `destandardize_channels` preprocess_fn, and the `[MODEL]`\n211\t   parameter count. This is also the first run of that path that does not hit the flatten bug.\n212\t\n213\t## Open questions for the user\n214\t\n215\t1. **Scope.** Model only, or the cached-feature study (\u00a74) and docs (\u00a75) too? Left as written,\n216\t   the plan does all of it so the study and the model measure the same thing.\n217\t2. **One scalar, or a small vector?** As specified this is exactly one number from AIA193,\n218\t   giving a 2-parameter monotone threshold detector. If more capacity is wanted while staying\n219\t   \"scalars, not images\", the natural extensions are one scalar per channel (13 features) or a\n220\t   few spatial statistics of the same pooled map (mean|\u00b7|, max|\u00b7|, 95th percentile \u2192 3-4\n221\t   features). Say which you want; the plan assumes the literal one-scalar version.\n222\t3. **Feature standardization.** Raw scalar into `nn.Linear(1,1)` (current plan), or standardize\n223\t   with train-split statistics? It changes optimization speed and calibration only, never AUROC.\n224\t4. **Keep the map variant?** The plan replaces it in the model and keeps it reachable in the\n225\t   study script for comparison. Alternative: a second class, or a `readout: {map, scalar}` flag.\n226\t",
