      "type": "msg",
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "The plan is written. Two things I found while reading that you should know before approving:\n\n**1. The current file is broken.** `simple_baseline.py:139-140` pools to `(B, 1, 128, 128)` and then calls `nn.Linear(16384, 1)` on it \u2014 the last dim is 128, so `--train_baseline` raises a shape error today. The stale `.pyc` still has `flatten(start_dim=\u2026)` in that forward and `1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb` has working output from it, so the flatten was dropped in the uncommitted edit. The scalar rewrite removes the need for it entirely.\n\n**2. `abs()` does not compose the way mean pooling does.** `wave_common.py` builds its whole caching strategy on \"pool 8 then repool to 32 is exact.\" That stays true, but `mean(|pool8|) \u2260 mean(|pool32|)`, so the abs has to be applied *after* repooling to kernel 32 and *before* the spatial mean. Get that order wrong and the cached-feature study silently measures a different quantity than the model trains on. The good news: the existing pool-8 cache still serves every kernel exactly, so no re-extraction pass.\n\nOne consequence worth flagging: a logistic regression on **one** feature is monotone in that feature, so AUROC becomes a fixed property of the feature \u2014 computable in closed form without training. Only the loss and threshold are learned. It also means `lr: 0.01` is likely too small: the scalar is order 0.1 while the weight needs to reach order 10-100, and Adam moves a parameter by ~`lr` per step. The plan handles that.\n\n**Questions:**\n\n1. **Scope** \u2014 model only, or the cached-feature study (`run_baseline_study.py`) and the docs too? The plan does all of it, so the study and the model measure the same thing.\n2. **One scalar, or a small vector?** As you described it, this is exactly one number from AIA193 \u2192 a 2-parameter threshold detector. If you want more capacity while staying off images: one scalar per channel (13 features), or a few statistics of the same pooled map (mean|\u00b7|, max|\u00b7|, p95 \u2192 3-4 features).\n3. **Standardization** \u2014 z-score the feature in the study script and raise the lr for the Lightning arm (keeps the model a clean 2-parameter object), or bake a fixed `(mean, std)` into the model so both paths are identical?\n4. **Whole frame, or disk only?** The pooled map spans all 4096\u00b2, and the disk is ~40% of it. Averaging over everything dilutes the wave with ~60% off-disk pixels \u2014 the constant part is absorbed by the weight, but the varying part is pure noise that can only lower AUROC. `wave_visibility_scan.py` already has a `disk_mask`. The plan follows your description literally and does **not** mask; say so and it will.\n5. **Keep the map variant?** Plan replaces it in the model, keeps it reachable in the study script for an A/B.\n\nAlso worth knowing: `wave_common.py:25` and the experiments README both promise an `assert_feature_matches_model()` that doesn't exist \u2014 which is precisely why the dropped flatten went unnoticed. The plan implements it as the cache/model consistency check.",
      "time": 1789673006.4805682,
